public class _1B_BruteForce_O_n2 {

    /*
    Interview : Given a string containing only a, b, and c, find the number of
                substrings that contain at least one a, one b, and one c.

    Given : We need to count every contiguous substring that contains all
            three characters at least once.

            Contiguous + Size is Not Fixed + Contains a,b,c + SubString = Variable Window
            subString = window

    Brain : Generate every possible substring using two loops.

            i → starting position of the substring.
            j → ending position of the substring.

            We do not need frequency because the solution only depends on
            whether a, b, and c are present inside the current substring.

            Since the string contains only a, b, and c, three boolean variables
            are enough to track whether all three required characters exist.

    Idea : A. Normal :

               Generate every possible substring and check whether it contains
               all three characters using three boolean variables.

               We do not care how many times each character occurs because
               the requirement only asks whether each character exists.

               T = O(n²)

           B. Optimal :

               Store the latest index of a, b, and c while moving R forward.

               The minimum latest index tells us how many starting positions
               can create valid substrings ending at the current R.

               T = O(n)
               S = O(1)
    */

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String str = "abcabc";

        /* Step 0 : Variables analogy for brute force :

                    a. 'i' selects the starting position of the substring.
                    b. 'j' selects the ending position of the substring.
                    c. 'hasA' tracks whether character a exists in the substring.
                    d. 'hasB' tracks whether character b exists in the substring.
                    e. 'hasC' tracks whether character c exists in the substring.
                    f. 'ans' stores the total number of valid substrings. */

        int ans = 0;


        /* Step 1 : Select every possible starting position using the outer loop. */

        for(int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {

            boolean hasA = false;
            boolean hasB = false;
            boolean hasC = false;


            /* Step 2 : Extend the substring one character at a time using J. */

            for(int j = i; j < str.length(); j++) {

                char current = str.charAt(j);


                /* Step 3 : Mark the character as present because we only need
                            to know whether each required character exists. */

                if(current == 'a') {
                    hasA = true;
                }
                else if(current == 'b') {
                    hasB = true;
                }
                else {
                    hasC = true;
                }


                /* Step 4 : If all three characters are present, the current
                            substring satisfies the problem requirement. */

                if(hasA && hasB && hasC) {
                    ans++;
                }
            }
        }


        System.out.println(ans);

        /*
        Complexity :

        Time = O(n²)
        Reason : The outer loop selects every starting position, while the inner
                 loop checks every possible ending position for each starting position.

        Space = O(1)
        Reason : Only three boolean variables are used to track character presence,
                 so the extra memory remains constant regardless of the input length.
        */

        System.out.println("Time = O(n²), Space = O(1)");
    }
}


